  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Tuesday)\nIssued at 151 PM CDT WED JUL 22 2026\n\nOver the next few days, a steady stream of Gulf moisture will\ncontinue flowing into the region, remaining influenced by TS\nBertha. During this same timeframe, the cold front that we've\nbeen mentioning the last few days will finally come to a halt,\ngetting removed from the best forcing aloft. This stalled front,\npaired with the steady flow of moisture, will set the stage for\ncontinuous rounds of heavy showers and thunderstorms through the\nweekend. While the heaviest bands of rainfall appear to be to our\nnorth, there is at least medium confidence in these heavy bands\nshifting south into the afternoon on Friday. Given the high\nrainfall rates, and slow moving nature of these storms, we have\ngone ahead and issued a Flood Watch, valid until 7 AM Saturday.\nThis Flood Watch may get expanded in subsequent shifts, but the\noverall scope of this event depends on how far south the cold\nfront ends up stalling.\n\nThankfully, temperatures still look to cool down past today, with\nafternoon highs falling into the upper-80s and low-90s given the\ncold front and increased rainfall. This is where afternoon highs\nare expected to stay through the weekend, before climbing once\nmore by the start of next week. Here, the upper-level ridge to\nour west will begin to build east, resulting in the return of mid\nto high-90s. Confidence is already high that given this scenario,\nwe'll need some type of heat headlines to start the new workweek.\n\n/44/",
